> So, if I've got, say, 40K in gold, 5
> amulets, 5 artifact weapons, some spare boots and helmets and cloaks,
> 20 scrolls, 10 rings, the high-payoff food (lembas, candy, jelly,
> pancakes), 5 crucial spellbooks, 20 wands (mostly digging, teleport,
> and sleep), and an assortment of tools including a pickaxe and a
> blessed tinning kit, that makes my BoH pretty darn heavy right there.

You should cash your money at the local priest, not carry it around.
You can wear only one amulet: a single spare one for accidents is
enough.
Boots, helmets and cloaks don't weight a lot, and you shouldn't be
 carrying many in any case. Backups belong in stashes: carry only
special
 ones like boots of levitation or water walking.
Neither do scrolls and rings.
One emergency ration should be plenty: corpses are enough to feed you.
Spellbooks can easily be left behind.
You only need wands of digging at the earth level: leave them by the
 stairs.
Wands of teleportation *are* useful once you start the final run. Until
 then, leave them behind and walk. There's no hurry (yet).
The pickaxe and tinning kit are indeed very useful, and usually in my
 main directory as well. Other tools don't weigh very much.

If you follow the above instructions, your carrying capacity problems
will almost certainly disappear.

